
110 Problem:-
Given a binary tree, determine if it is height-balanced.

Example 1:
Input: root = [3,9,20,null,null,15,7]
Output: true

Example 2:
Input: root = [1,2,2,3,3,null,null,4,4]
Output: false

Example 3:
Input: root = []
Output: true
 
Constraints:-
The number of nodes in the tree is in the range [0, 5000].
-104 <= Node.val <= 104

=============================================================================================================================================================

Solution:-
Table of Contents:-
Intuition
Approach
Complexity
 
Intuition:-
The problem asks us to determine if a binary tree is height-balanced. A tree is balanced if the height difference between the left and right subtrees of every node is at most 1.
A naive approach would be to calculate the height of the left and right subtrees for every node from the top down. However, calculating height takes O(N),
and doing it for every node results in an inefficient O(N 2) time complexity.
We can optimize this by using a Bottom-Up DFS approach. Instead of just returning the height, our DFS function can return a special sentinel value (like -1) 
if it detects that a subtree is unbalanced. This allows us to validate the tree in a single pass.

Approach:-
We implement a helper function dfs(node) that returns the height of the tree rooted at node, or -1 if it is unbalanced.
Base Case: If node is null, return 0 (height of an empty tree).
Recursive Step (Left): Recursively call dfs on the left child.
Optimization: If it returns -1, it means the left subtree is already unbalanced. We immediately return -1 (pruning) without checking the right side.
Recursive Step (Right): Recursively call dfs on the right child.
If it returns -1, return -1.
Check Balance: If the absolute difference between the left and right height is greater than 1, return -1.
Return Height: If balanced, return the actual height: 1 + max(left_height, right_height).
Finally, in the main function, we simply check if dfs(root) is not -1.

Complexity:-
Time complexity: O(N)
We traverse every node exactly once. The "early exit" optimization helps skip unnecessary calculations.
Space complexity: O(H)
Where H is the height of the tree (recursion stack). In the worst case (skewed tree), it is O(N); in a balanced tree, it is O(logN).

Code:-
class Solution {
    public boolean isBalanced(TreeNode root) {
        return dfsHeight(root) != -1;
    }

    private int dfsHeight(TreeNode node) {
        if (node == null) return 0;

        int leftHeight = dfsHeight(node.left);
        if (leftHeight == -1) return -1;

        int rightHeight = dfsHeight(node.right);
        if (rightHeight == -1) return -1;

        if (Math.abs(leftHeight - rightHeight) > 1) {
            return -1;
        }

        return 1 + Math.max(leftHeight, rightHeight);
    }
}
